: en","messages":[{"role":"user","content":"Write the article based on this outline plan:\n\nPlan JSON:\n{\n  \"title\": \"Docker Compose A-Z: Complete Guide for Developers 2026\",\n  \"difficulty\": \"beginner\",\n  \"estimatedReadingTime\": 10,\n  \"keywords\": [\n    \"Docker Compose\",\n    \"Docker\",\n    \"containerization\",\n    \"multi-container applications\",\n    \"DevOps tools\",\n    \"YAML configuration\"\n  ],\n  \"outline\": [\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Introduction to Docker Compose\",\n      \"description\": \"Explain what Docker Compose is, its purpose in managing multi-container Docker applications, and its core benefits for developers, especially in development and testing environments. Briefly introduce key concepts like services, networks, and volumes.\",\n      \"level\": 2\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Prerequisites and Installation\",\n      \"description\": \"Detail the necessary prerequisites, primarily ensuring Docker Engine is installed and running. Provide step-by-step instructions for installing Docker Compose on various operating systems (Windows, macOS, Linux), including verifying the installation.\",\n      \"level\": 2\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Understanding the `docker-compose.yml` File\",\n      \"description\": \"Break down the structure and syntax of the `docker-compose.yml` file. Explain essential top-level keys such as `version`, `services`, `networks`, and `volumes`. Provide clear explanations for common directives within each section.\",\n      \"level\": 2\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Your First Docker Compose Project\",\n      \"description\": \"Guide the reader through creating a simple multi-container application from scratch. This section will focus on practical application and hands-on experience.\",\n      \"level\": 2\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Setting up a Simple Web Application\",\n      \"description\": \"Walk through creating a basic web application (e.g., a Python Flask app or Node.js app) and a web server (e.g., Nginx) using Docker Compose. Include creating a `Dockerfile` for the application and configuring the `docker-compose.yml` to link them.\",\n      \"level\": 3\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Adding a Database Service\",\n      \"description\": \"Extend the previous example by integrating a database service (e.g., PostgreSQL or MySQL). Explain how to configure environment variables for database connection and define a named volume for data persistence.\",\n      \"level\": 3\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Essential Docker Compose Commands\",\n      \"description\": \"Introduce and explain the most frequently used Docker Compose commands. Provide examples for each command to demonstrate their functionality.\",\n      \"level\": 2\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"`docker compose up` and `docker compose down`\",\n      \"description\": \"Explain how to start and stop all services defined in the `docker-compose.yml` file, including options for detached mode (`-d`) and rebuilding images (`--build`).\",\n      \"level\": 3\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"`docker compose ps`, `logs`, and `exec`\",\n      \"description\": \"Cover commands for inspecting the status of services, viewing logs, and executing commands inside running service containers for debugging or maintenance.\",\n      \"level\": 3\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"`docker compose build` and `pull`\",\n      \"description\": \"Explain how to explicitly build service images or pull them from a registry.\",\n      \"level\": 3\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Advanced Docker Compose Features\",\n      \"description\": \"Delve into more sophisticated features that enhance flexibility, reusability, and environment management.\",\n      \"level\": 2\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Environment Variables and `.env` Files\",\n      \"description\": \"Demonstrate how to manage environment variables within Docker Compose, including using `.env` files for sensitive data or configuration.\",\n      \"level\": 3\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Build Context and Arguments\",\n      \"description\": \"Explain how to customize image builds using build contexts and build arguments within the `docker-compose.yml`.\",\n      \"level\": 3\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Extending Services with `extends`\",\n      \"description\": \"Show how to reuse common service configurations across multiple `docker-compose.yml` files using the `extends` keyword.\",\n      \"level\": 3\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Profiles for Different Environments\",\n      \"description\": \"Introduce Docker Compose profiles as a way to manage different sets of services for various environments (e.g., development, testing, production).\",\n      \"level\": 3\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Health Checks\",\n      \"description\": \"Explain how to configure health checks for services to ensure they are running and responsive before dependent services start.\",\n      \"level\": 3\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Best Practices for Docker Compose\",\n      \"description\": \"Provide guidance on structuring Docker Compose projects, managing secrets, optimizing performance, and implementing security considerations for robust applications.\",\n      \"level\": 2\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Troubleshooting Common Issues\",\n      \"description\": \"Address frequently encountered problems when working with Docker Compose, such as port conflicts, network issues, volume mounting errors, and service startup failures, offering solutions and debugging tips.\",\n      \"level\": 2\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Conclusion and Next Steps\",\n      \"description\": \"Summarize the key benefits and capabilities of Docker Compose. Offer suggestions for further learning, such as exploring Docker Swarm, Kubernetes, or more complex multi-service architectures.\",\n      \"level\": 2\n    }\n  ]\n}\n\nAdditional Instructions:\nExplain what Docker Compose is, its purpose in managing multi-container Docker applications, and its core benefits for developers, especially in development and testing environments. Briefly introduce key concepts like services, networks, and volumes.\n\n"}]},"executionTimeMs":17749,"success":true,"response":"## Introduction to Docker Compose\n\nIn the modern landscape of software development, applications are rarely monolithic. Instead, they are often composed of multiple interconnected services—a web server, an application backend, a database, a cache, and more. While Docker excels at containerizing individual services, orchestrating these multiple containers to work together can become complex. This is where Docker Compose steps in.\n\nDocker Compose is a tool for defining and running multi-container Docker applications. With Compose, you use a YAML file to configure your application's services. Then, with a single command, you create and start all the services from your configuration.\n\nIts primary purpose is to simplify the development, testing, and staging workflows for multi-container applications. Developers can define their entire application stack in a single file, ensuring consistency across different environments and making it easy for new team members to get started.\n\nCore benefits for developers include:\n*   **Simplified Setup**: Define your entire application stack (services, networks, volumes) in one `docker-compose.yml` file.\n*   **Consistent Environments**: Ensure that every developer, as well as CI/CD pipelines, runs the exact same environment.\n*   **Rapid Development**: Start, stop, and rebuild your entire application with simple commands, accelerating the development cycle.\n*   **Isolation**: Each service runs in its own isolated container, preventing conflicts and making debugging easier.\n*   **Portability**: Your application stack can be easily moved and run on any machine with Docker and Docker Compose installed.\n\nKey concepts within Docker Compose include:\n*   **Services**: These are the individual containers that make up your application (e.g., a web app, a database, a message queue). Each service is defined by an image and configuration options.\n*   **Networks**: Compose automatically creates a default network for your application, allowing services to communicate with each other by their service names. You can also define custom networks.\n*   **Volumes**: Used for persisting data generated by or used by Docker containers. This ensures that your data isn't lost when containers are stopped or removed.\n\n## Prerequisites and Installation\n\nBefore you can start using Docker Compose, you need to have Docker Engine installed and running on your system. Docker Compose relies on the Docker Engine to build and run containers.\n\n### Installing Docker Engine\n\nIf you don't have Docker Engine installed, please follow the official Docker documentation for your operating system:\n*   **Windows**: [Install Docker Desktop on Windows](https://docs.docker.com/desktop/install/windows-install/)\n*   **macOS**: [Install Docker Desktop on Mac](https://docs.docker.com/desktop/install/mac-install/)\n*   **Linux**: [Install Docker Engine on Linux](https://docs.docker.com/engine/install/)\n\nAfter installation, verify that Docker is running by opening a terminal or command prompt and executing:\n\n```bash\ndocker --version\ndocker run hello-world\n```\n\nYou should see output indicating the Docker version and a \"Hello from Docker!\" message, confirming that Docker is correctly installed and operational.\n\n### Installing Docker Compose\n\nAs of Docker Desktop 3.0+, Docker Compose is typically bundled with Docker Desktop for Windows and macOS. For Linux users, or if you're using an older Docker Desktop version, you might need to install it separately. The `docker-compose` command has been superseded by `docker compose` (without the hyphen) in newer versions, which is integrated directly into the Docker CLI. This guide will use the `docker compose` command.\n\n#### Windows and macOS (Docker Desktop)\n\nIf you have Docker Desktop installed, `docker compose` is already available. No separate installation is needed.\n\n#### Linux\n\nFor Linux, you can install the Docker Compose plugin via the Docker package manager or manually.\n\n**Method 1: Install using `apt` (for Debian/Ubuntu)**\n\n```bash\nsudo apt update\nsudo apt install docker-compose-plugin\n```\n\n**Method 2: Manual Installation (for other Linux distributions or if `apt` doesn't work)**\n\n```bash\n# Download the current stable release of Docker Compose\nsudo curl -L https://github.com/docker/compose/releases/latest/download/docker-compose-linux-x86_64 -o /usr/local/bin/docker-compose\n\n# Apply executable permissions to the binary\nsudo chmod +x /usr/local/bin/docker-compose\n\n# For the new `docker compose` command (plugin version)\n# Ensure the plugin directory exists\nmkdir -p ~/.docker/cli-plugins/\n# Download the plugin\ncurl -SL https://github.com/docker/compose/releases/latest/download/docker-compose-linux-x86_64 -o ~/.docker/cli-plugins/docker-compose\nchmod +x ~/.docker/cli-plugins/docker-compose\n```\nNote: The manual installation for `docker-compose` (with hyphen) is for older versions. For the integrated `docker compose` (without hyphen) command, the plugin method is preferred.\n\n### Verifying the Installation\n\nAfter installation, verify that Docker Compose is correctly installed by running:\n\n```bash\ndocker compose version\n```\n\nYou should see output indicating the Docker Compose version, confirming that it's ready to use.\n\n## Understanding the `docker-compose.yml` File\n\nThe heart of any Docker Compose project is the `docker-compose.yml` file. This YAML-formatted file defines your application's services, networks, and volumes. It's declarative, meaning you describe the desired state of your application, and Docker Compose handles making it a reality.\n\n### Structure and Syntax\n\nA `docker-compose.yml` file typically starts with a `version` key, followed by top-level keys like `services`, `networks`, and `volumes`.\n\n```yaml\nversion: '3.8' # Specifies the Compose file format version\n\nservices:\n  # Define your application services here\n\nnetworks:\n  # Define custom networks here (optional)\n\nvolumes:\n  # Define named volumes here (optional)\n```\n\n### Essential Top-Level Keys\n\n#### `version`\n\nThis key is mandatory and specifies the Compose file format version. Using a newer version (e.g., `'3.8'`) grants access to more features and better syntax.\n\n```yaml\nversion: '3.8'\n```\n\n#### `services`\n\nThis is the most important section, where you define each individual container that forms part of your application. Each key under `services` represents a service, and its value is a dictionary of configuration options for that service.\n\n```yaml\nservices:\n  web: # Name of the service\n    image: nginx:latest # Docker image to use\n    ports:\n      - \"80:80\" # Port mapping: host_port:container_port\n    volumes:\n      - ./nginx.conf:/etc/nginx/nginx.conf # Volume mount: host_path:container_path\n    depends_on:\n      - app # Specify dependencies on other services\n    networks:\n      - my_app_network # Assign to a custom network\n\n  app:\n    build: . # Build an image from a Dockerfile in the current directory\n    environment:\n      DATABASE_URL: postgres://user:password@db:5432/mydb # Environment variables\n    networks:\n      - my_app_network\n\n  db:\n    image: postgres:13\n    environment:\n      POSTGRES_DB: mydb\n      POSTGRES_USER: user\n      POSTGRES_PASSWORD: password\n    volumes:\n      - db_data:/var/lib/postgresql/data # Named volume for persistence\n    networks:\n      - my_app_network\n```\n\nCommon directives within a service definition:\n*   **`image`**: Specifies the Docker image to use for the service (e.g., `nginx:latest`, `postgres:13`).\n*   **`build`**: Instead of pulling an image, build it from a `Dockerfile` located at the specified path (e.g., `.` for the current directory).\n*   **`ports`**: Maps ports from the host to the container (e.g., `\"80:80\"` maps host port 80 to container port 80).\n*   **`volumes`**: Mounts host paths or named volumes into the container for data persistence or configuration (e.g., `./data:/app/data`, `db_data:/var/lib/postgresql/data`).\n*   **`environment`**: Sets environment variables inside the container. Useful for configuration (e.g., `DATABASE_URL`).\n*   **`depends_on`**: Expresses dependencies between services. Services listed here will be started before the current service. Note: This only ensures startup order, not service readiness.\n*   **`networks`**: Attaches the service to one or more custom networks defined in the `networks` section.\n*   **`restart`**: Defines the restart policy for the container (e.g., `always`, `on-failure`, `no`).\n*   **`command`**: Overrides the default command defined in the image's `Dockerfile`.\n*   **`entrypoint`**: Overrides the default entrypoint defined in the image's `Dockerfile`.\n\n#### `networks`\n\nThis section allows you to define custom networks that your services can connect to. By default, Docker Compose creates a single bridge network for your application, allowing all services to communicate. Custom networks provide better isolation and organization.\n\n```yaml\nnetworks:\n  my_app_network:\n    driver: bridge # Default driver, can be omitted\n  public_network:\n    external: true # Connect to an existing external network\n```\n\n#### `volumes`\n\nThis section defines named volumes, which are the preferred way to persist data generated by Docker containers. Named volumes are managed by Docker and are more robust for data persistence than bind mounts (mounting host paths directly).\n\n```yaml\nvolumes:\n  db_data: # A named volume for database data\n  app_logs: # Another named volume for application logs\n```\n\n## Your First Docker Compose Project\n\nLet's get hands-on and build a simple multi-container application using Docker Compose. We'll start with a basic web application and then integrate a database.\n\n### Setting up a Simple Web Application\n\nWe'll create a simple Python Flask application served by Nginx.\n\n1.  **Create a project directory**:\n    ```bash\n    mkdir my-flask-nginx-app\n    cd my-flask-nginx-app\n    ```\n\n2.  **Create the Flask application (`app.py`)**:\n    ```python\n    # app.py\n    from flask import Flask\n    import os\n\n    app = Flask(__name__)\n\n    @app.route('/')\n    def hello():\n        return f\"Hello from Flask app! Running on port {os.environ.get('PORT', '5000')}\"\n\n    if __name__ == '__main__':\n        app.run(host='0.0.0.0', port=5000)\n    ```\n\n3.  **Create `requirements.txt`**:\n    ```\n    Flask==2.3.3\n    ```\n\n4.  **Create a `Dockerfile` for the Flask app**:\n    ```dockerfile\n    # Dockerfile\n    FROM python:3.9-slim-buster\n\n    WORKDIR /app\n\n    COPY requirements.txt .\n    RUN pip install --no-cache-dir -r requirements.txt\n\n    COPY . .\n\n    EXPOSE 5000\n\n    CMD [\"python\", \"app.py\"]\n    ```\n\n5.  **Create an Nginx configuration file (`nginx.conf`)**:\n    ```nginx\n    # nginx.conf\n    events {\n        worker_connections 1024;\n    }\n\n    http {\n        upstream flask_app {\n            server app:5000; # 'app' is the service name of our Flask app\n        }\n\n        server {\n            listen 80;\n\n            location / {\n                proxy_pass http://flask_app;\n                proxy_set_header Host $host;\n                proxy_set_header X-Real-IP $remote_addr;\n                proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;\n                proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Proto $scheme;\n            }\n        }\n    }\n    ```\n\n6.  **Create the `docker-compose.yml` file**:\n    ```yaml\n    # docker-compose.yml\n    version: '3.8'\n\n    services:\n      web:\n        image: nginx:latest\n        ports:\n          - \"80","responseLength":11404,"tokenUsage":{"inputTokens":1749,"outputTokens":2748,"totalTokens":5531},"estimatedCost":0.001911,"promptName":"writer","promptVersion":"v2"}
{"id":"69e4df30-35ec-42be-9d36-8b199ecb782d","timestamp":"2026-07-25T09:37:47.588Z","providerId":"gemini","model":"quality","prompt":{"system":"# ROLE\nYou are a Professional Technical Writer. Your goal is to draft a comprehensive, detailed technical article in Markdown based on the provided planner outline plan.\n\nCRITICAL OPERATIONAL RULES:\n\n1. OUTLINE INTEGRITY (Why: Prevents outline drift and ensures the draft matches the user-approved structure):\n   - Follow the planned outline structure exactly.\n   - Do not add new headings, omit headings, rename headings, or change the order of sections.\n   - Every heading listed in the plan MUST exist as a heading inside the output markdown text.\n\n2. FORMATTING RULES (Why: Ensures standard SEO and web styling hierarchies):\n   - Write the text using clean Markdown.\n   - Use ## for main sections (level 2) and ### for sub-sections (level 3). Do not use H1 (#) as it is reserved for the main post title.\n   - Wrap all code blocks with clean language tags (e.g. ```typescript ... ``` or ```bash ... ```) to enable syntax highlighting.\n\n3. INFORMATION TRUTH (Why: Halts hallucinations and fake documentation parameters):\n   - Explain complex technical concepts clearly with practical, real-world examples.\n   - Do not hallucinate package names, non-existent API endpoints, configuration fields, or library options.\n   - Do not generate fake references, URLs, links, or bibliography sections.\n\n4. SCOPE BOUNDARIES (Why: Prevents metadata pollution — SEO fields and slugs are generated by dedicated downstream agents):\n   - Do not generate SEO meta description blocks, slugs, categories, tags, or keyword lists inside the markdown.\n   - Focus 100% of your output on the actual article body copy.\n\n5. OUTPUT FORMAT (Why: Enforces standard raw Markdown streaming content):\n   - Output the raw Markdown content directly. Do not wrap in JSON. Do not write markdown fences around the entire response.\n\n# CONTEXT\n- **Target Language**: en","messages":[{"role":"user","content":"Write the article based on this outline plan:\n\nPlan JSON:\n{\n  \"title\": \"Docker Compose A-Z: Complete Guide for Developers 2026\",\n  \"difficulty\": \"beginner\",\n  \"estimatedReadingTime\": 10,\n  \"keywords\": [\n    \"Docker Compose\",\n    \"Docker\",\n    \"containerization\",\n    \"multi-container applications\",\n    \"DevOps tools\",\n    \"YAML configuration\"\n  ],\n  \"outline\": [\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Introduction to Docker Compose\",\n      \"description\": \"Explain what Docker Compose is, its purpose in managing multi-container Docker applications, and its core benefits for developers, especially in development and testing environments. Briefly introduce key concepts like services, networks, and volumes.\",\n      \"level\": 2\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Prerequisites and Installation\",\n      \"description\": \"Detail the necessary prerequisites, primarily ensuring Docker Engine is installed and running. Provide step-by-step instructions for installing Docker Compose on various operating systems (Windows, macOS, Linux), including verifying the installation.\",\n      \"level\": 2\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Understanding the `docker-compose.yml` File\",\n      \"description\": \"Break down the structure and syntax of the `docker-compose.yml` file. Explain essential top-level keys such as `version`, `services`, `networks`, and `volumes`. Provide clear explanations for common directives within each section.\",\n      \"level\": 2\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Your First Docker Compose Project\",\n      \"description\": \"Guide the reader through creating a simple multi-container application from scratch. This section will focus on practical application and hands-on experience.\",\n      \"level\": 2\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Setting up a Simple Web Application\",\n      \"description\": \"Walk through creating a basic web application (e.g., a Python Flask app or Node.js app) and a web server (e.g., Nginx) using Docker Compose. Include creating a `Dockerfile` for the application and configuring the `docker-compose.yml` to link them.\",\n      \"level\": 3\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Adding a Database Service\",\n      \"description\": \"Extend the previous example by integrating a database service (e.g., PostgreSQL or MySQL). Explain how to configure environment variables for database connection and define a named volume for data persistence.\",\n      \"level\": 3\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Essential Docker Compose Commands\",\n      \"description\": \"Introduce and explain the most frequently used Docker Compose commands. Provide examples for each command to demonstrate their functionality.\",\n      \"level\": 2\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"`docker compose up` and `docker compose down`\",\n      \"description\": \"Explain how to start and stop all services defined in the `docker-compose.yml` file, including options for detached mode (`-d`) and rebuilding images (`--build`).\",\n      \"level\": 3\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"`docker compose ps`, `logs`, and `exec`\",\n      \"description\": \"Cover commands for inspecting the status of services, viewing logs, and executing commands inside running service containers for debugging or maintenance.\",\n      \"level\": 3\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"`docker compose build` and `pull`\",\n      \"description\": \"Explain how to explicitly build service images or pull them from a registry.\",\n      \"level\": 3\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Advanced Docker Compose Features\",\n      \"description\": \"Delve into more sophisticated features that enhance flexibility, reusability, and environment management.\",\n      \"level\": 2\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Environment Variables and `.env` Files\",\n      \"description\": \"Demonstrate how to manage environment variables within Docker Compose, including using `.env` files for sensitive data or configuration.\",\n      \"level\": 3\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Build Context and Arguments\",\n      \"description\": \"Explain how to customize image builds using build contexts and build arguments within the `docker-compose.yml`.\",\n      \"level\": 3\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Extending Services with `extends`\",\n      \"description\": \"Show how to reuse common service configurations across multiple `docker-compose.yml` files using the `extends` keyword.\",\n      \"level\": 3\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Profiles for Different Environments\",\n      \"description\": \"Introduce Docker Compose profiles as a way to manage different sets of services for various environments (e.g., development, testing, production).\",\n      \"level\": 3\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Health Checks\",\n      \"description\": \"Explain how to configure health checks for services to ensure they are running and responsive before dependent services start.\",\n      \"level\": 3\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Best Practices for Docker Compose\",\n      \"description\": \"Provide guidance on structuring Docker Compose projects, managing secrets, optimizing performance, and implementing security considerations for robust applications.\",\n      \"level\": 2\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Troubleshooting Common Issues\",\n      \"description\": \"Address frequently encountered problems when working with Docker Compose, such as port conflicts, network issues, volume mounting errors, and service startup failures, offering solutions and debugging tips.\",\n      \"level\": 2\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Conclusion and Next Steps\",\n      \"description\": \"Summarize the key benefits and capabilities of Docker Compose. Offer suggestions for further learning, such as exploring Docker Swarm, Kubernetes, or more complex multi-service architectures.\",\n      \"level\": 2\n    }\n  ]\n}\n\nAdditional Instructions:\nExplain what Docker Compose is, its purpose in managing multi-container Docker applications, and its core benefits for developers, especially in development and testing environments. Briefly introduce key concepts like services, networks, and volumes.\n\n"}]},"executionTimeMs":19123,"success":true,"response":"## Introduction to Docker Compose\n\nIn the world of containerization, Docker has revolutionized how developers build, ship, and run applications. While Docker excels at managing individual containers, real-world applications often consist of multiple interconnected services—a web server, an application backend, a database, a cache, and so on. This is where Docker Compose steps in.\n\nDocker Compose is a tool for defining and running multi-container Docker applications. With Compose, you use a YAML file to configure your application's services, networks, and volumes. Then, with a single command, you can create and start all the services from your configuration.\n\nIts primary purpose is to simplify the orchestration of complex applications composed of several Docker containers. Instead of manually starting each container, configuring their links, and managing their lifecycles, Docker Compose automates this entire process.\n\n**Core Benefits for Developers:**\n\n*   **Simplified Development Workflows:** Developers can define their entire application stack in a single file, making it easy to spin up a consistent development environment on any machine.\n*   **Consistent Environments:** Ensures that development, testing, and even staging environments closely mirror each other, reducing \"it works on my machine\" issues.\n*   **Rapid Iteration:** Quickly rebuild, restart, and scale services during development without complex manual commands.\n*   **Easy Collaboration:** Share your `docker-compose.yml` file with teammates, and they can get the entire application running with minimal effort.\n*   **Testing:** Ideal for setting up integration testing environments where multiple services need to interact.\n\n**Key Concepts Introduced:**\n\n*   **Services:** Individual containers that make up your application (e.g., a web app, a database, a Redis cache). Each service is defined by an image and configuration.\n*   **Networks:** Docker Compose sets up a default network for your application, allowing services to communicate with each other using their service names as hostnames.\n*   **Volumes:** Used for persisting data generated by or used by Docker containers. This ensures that your data isn't lost when containers are stopped or removed.\n\n## Prerequisites and Installation\n\nBefore you can use Docker Compose, you must have Docker Engine installed and running on your system. Docker Compose relies on the Docker Engine to build and run containers.\n\n### Installing Docker Engine\n\nFollow the official Docker documentation for installing Docker Engine on your specific operating system:\n\n*   **Windows & macOS:** Install [Docker Desktop](https://www.docker.com/products/docker-desktop), which includes Docker Engine, Docker CLI, Docker Compose, and Kubernetes.\n*   **Linux:** Follow the distribution-specific instructions (e.g., for Ubuntu, CentOS, Fedora) from the [Docker Engine installation guide](https://docs.docker.com/engine/install/).\n\n### Installing Docker Compose\n\nFor modern Docker installations (Docker Desktop for Windows/macOS, or Docker Engine 1.13.0+ on Linux), Docker Compose is typically bundled as part of the Docker CLI, accessible via the `docker compose` command (note the space).\n\n**Verifying Installation:**\n\nAfter installing Docker Desktop or Docker Engine, open your terminal or command prompt and run:\n\n```bash\ndocker compose version\n```\n\nYou should see output similar to this, indicating that Compose is installed and its version:\n\n```\nDocker Compose version v2.23.3\n```\n\nIf you see `docker-compose version` (with a hyphen) and it works, you might be using an older, standalone version of Docker Compose. While still functional, the `docker compose` (with a space) syntax is the recommended approach for newer Docker versions. This guide will primarily use the `docker compose` (with a space) syntax.\n\n## Understanding the `docker-compose.yml` File\n\nThe `docker-compose.yml` file is the heart of any Docker Compose project. It's a YAML-formatted file that describes your application's services, networks, and volumes.\n\n### Structure and Syntax\n\nA typical `docker-compose.yml` file starts with a `version` key, followed by top-level keys like `services`, `networks`, and `volumes`.\n\n```yaml\nversion: '3.8' # Specifies the Compose file format version\nservices:\n  # Define your application services here\n  web:\n    image: nginx:latest\n    ports:\n      - \"80:80\"\n  db:\n    image: postgres:13\n    environment:\n      POSTGRES_DB: mydatabase\n      POSTGRES_USER: user\n      POSTGRES_PASSWORD: password\nvolumes:\n  # Define named volumes here\n  db_data:\nnetworks:\n  # Define custom networks here\n  app_net:\n    driver: bridge\n```\n\n### Essential Top-Level Keys\n\n#### `version`\n\nThis key specifies the Compose file format version. It's crucial for defining which features are available and how they behave.\n*   `'3.8'` (or any version 3.x) is the most common and recommended version, offering the latest features.\n\n#### `services`\n\nThis is the most important section, where you define each individual container that forms part of your application. Each key under `services` represents a service name (e.g., `web`, `db`).\n\nCommon directives within a service:\n\n*   **`image`**: Specifies the Docker image to use for the service (e.g., `nginx:latest`, `postgres:13`). If not specified, Compose looks for a `build` directive.\n*   **`build`**: Instead of pulling an image, Compose will build it from a `Dockerfile`.\n    *   `build: .` (builds from a `Dockerfile` in the current directory).\n    *   `build: ./app` (builds from a `Dockerfile` in the `./app` directory).\n*   **`ports`**: Maps host ports to container ports.\n    *   `\"80:80\"` (maps host port 80 to container port 80).\n    *   `\"443\"` (exposes container port 443 on a random host port).\n*   **`environment`**: Sets environment variables inside the container.\n    *   `environment: - NODE_ENV=development`\n    *   `environment: POSTGRES_PASSWORD: password`\n*   **`volumes`**: Mounts host paths or named volumes into the container for data persistence or sharing.\n    *   `volumes: - ./app:/app` (mounts local `app` directory to container's `/app`).\n    *   `volumes: - db_data:/var/lib/postgresql/data` (mounts named volume `db_data`).\n*   **`depends_on`**: Expresses dependency between services. Compose starts services in dependency order. Note: this does not wait for the dependent service to be \"ready,\" only for it to be started.\n    *   `depends_on: - db - redis`\n*   **`networks`**: Connects a service to specified networks. By default, all services are connected to a default network.\n    *   `networks: - app_net`\n*   **`restart`**: Defines the restart policy for the container.\n    *   `restart: always` (always restart unless stopped)\n    *   `restart: on-failure` (restart only if the container exits with a non-zero status)\n\n#### `networks`\n\nThis section allows you to define custom networks for your services. This is useful for isolating services or creating specific network topologies.\n\n*   `app_net:` (defines a network named `app_net`).\n    *   `driver: bridge` (specifies the network driver, `bridge` is default).\n\n#### `volumes`\n\nThis section defines named volumes, which are the preferred way to persist data generated by and used by Docker containers. Named volumes are managed by Docker and are more robust than bind mounts for data persistence.\n\n*   `db_data:` (defines a named volume called `db_data`).\n    *   `driver: local` (specifies the volume driver, `local` is default).\n\n## Your First Docker Compose Project\n\nLet's create a simple multi-container application to demonstrate Docker Compose in action. We'll start with a basic web application and then integrate a database.\n\n### Setting up a Simple Web Application\n\nWe'll create a simple Python Flask application served by Nginx.\n\n1.  **Project Structure:**\n    Create a new directory for your project, e.g., `my-flask-app`.\n\n    ```\n    my-flask-app/\n    ├── app/\n    │   ├── app.py\n    │   └── Dockerfile\n    ├── nginx/\n    │   └── nginx.conf\n    └── docker-compose.yml\n    ```\n\n2.  **Flask Application (`app/app.py`):**\n    This is a minimal Flask app.\n\n    ```python\n    from flask import Flask\n    app = Flask(__name__)\n\n    @app.route('/')\n    def hello():\n        return \"Hello from Flask in a Docker container!\"\n\n    if __name__ == '__main__':\n        app.run(host='0.0.0.0', port=5000)\n    ```\n\n3.  **Flask Dockerfile (`app/Dockerfile`):**\n    This `Dockerfile` builds our Flask application image.\n\n    ```dockerfile\n    # Use an official Python runtime as a parent image\n    FROM python:3.9-slim-buster\n\n    # Set the working directory in the container\n    WORKDIR /app\n\n    # Copy the current directory contents into the container at /app\n    COPY . /app\n\n    # Install any needed packages specified in requirements.txt (if you had one)\n    # RUN pip install --no-cache-dir -r requirements.txt\n\n    # Install Flask\n    RUN pip install Flask\n\n    # Make port 5000 available to the world outside this container\n    EXPOSE 5000\n\n    # Run app.py when the container launches\n    CMD [\"python\", \"app.py\"]\n    ```\n\n4.  **Nginx Configuration (`nginx/nginx.conf`):**\n    Nginx will act as a reverse proxy for our Flask app.\n\n    ```nginx\n    events {\n        worker_connections 1024;\n    }\n\n    http {\n        server {\n            listen 80;\n            server_name localhost;\n\n            location / {\n                proxy_pass http://web:5000; # 'web' is the service name of our Flask app\n                proxy_set_header Host $host;\n                proxy_set_header X-Real-IP $remote_addr;\n                proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;\n                proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Proto $scheme;\n            }\n        }\n    }\n    ```\n    Notice `proxy_pass http://web:5000;` – `web` is the service name we'll define in `docker-compose.yml`. Docker Compose creates an internal network where services can resolve each other by their names.\n\n5.  **Docker Compose File (`docker-compose.yml`):**\n    This file defines our `web` (Flask) and `nginx` services.\n\n    ```yaml\n    version: '3.8'\n\n    services:\n      web:\n        build: ./app # Build from the Dockerfile in the 'app' directory\n        ports:\n          - \"5000:5000\" # Expose Flask app directly for testing, though N","responseLength":10268,"tokenUsage":{"inputTokens":1749,"outputTokens":2509,"totalTokens":5512},"estimatedCost":0.001768,"promptName":"writer","promptVersion":"v2"}
{"id":"a107f130-d193-47ea-a75f-90acf91f7cb5","timestamp":"2026-07-27T14:13:49.011Z","providerId":"default","model":"fast","prompt":{"system":"Execution Pipeline Block","messages":[{"role":"system","content":"# ROLE\nYou are an expert editorial planner. Your task is to plan a comprehensive technical article based on the provided topic, audience, goal, tone, length, and category.\n\nCRITICAL RULES:\n1. Output strictly valid JSON matching the schema below.\n2. Do not write any article paragraphs, content, introductions, or conclusions. Only generate the plan.\n3. All outline items must have heading level 2 (H2) or 3 (H3) based on depth. Do not use H1 (reserved for the title).\n4. Keywords must include 1 primary SEO target keyword and 3-5 LSI (latent semantic) secondary keywords.\n5. The estimated reading time must be a single integer in minutes, calculated at an average reading speed of 200 words per minute.\n6. The response must contain ONLY the raw JSON object. Do not wrap the JSON in Markdown code fences (e.g., do not use ```json ... ```).\n\nJSON Schema Output Structure:\n{\n  \"title\": \"string (SEO optimized title, max 60 chars)\",\n  \"difficulty\": \"beginner\" | \"intermediate\" | \"advanced\",\n  \"estimatedReadingTime\": number (integer in minutes),\n  \"keywords\": [\"string\", \"string\"],\n  \"outline\": [\n    {\n      \"title\": \"string (clear, descriptive heading)\",\n      \"description\": \"string (detailed description of what this section should cover)\",\n      \"level\": 2 | 3\n    }\n  ]\n}\n\n# CONTEXT\n- **Target Language**: en"},{"role":"user","content":"Generate an article plan for the following inputs:\n\n- Topic: 15 Spring Boot Best Practices giúp ứng dụng sẵn sàng cho Production\n- Target Audience: Intermediate Java Developers\n- Objective/Goal: Share practical knowledge and best practices\nSpecial: write by Vietnamese\n- Desired Tone: Professional & Technical\n- Target Length: Long (~2,500 words)\n- Subject Category: Backend Development"}]},"executionTimeMs":21261,"success":true,"response":"{\"title\":\"15 Best Practices Spring Boot Giúp Ứng Dụng Sẵn Sàng Production\",\"difficulty\":\"intermediate\",\"estimatedReadingTime\":13,\"keywords\":[\"Thực hành tốt Spring Boot\",\"Spring Boot Production\",\"Java Backend\",\"Microservices\",\"Tối ưu hiệu năng\",\"Bảo mật Spring Boot\"],\"outline\":[{\"title\":\"Giới thiệu\",\"description\":\"Tổng quan về tầm quan trọng của việc áp dụng các thực hành tốt trong phát triển ứng dụng Spring Boot để đảm bảo tính ổn định, hiệu suất và bảo mật khi triển khai lên môi trường production.\",\"level\":2},{\"title\":\"15 Thực Hành Tốt Spring Boot Giúp Ứng Dụng Sẵn Sàng Production\",\"description\":\"Phần chính trình bày chi tiết 15 thực hành tốt quan trọng nhất cho các ứng dụng Spring Boot.\",\"level\":2},{\"title\":\"1. Cấu hình Externalized (Externalized Configuration)\",\"description\":\"Giải thích cách sử dụng Spring Cloud Config, Environment Variables, Profile-specific properties để tách biệt cấu hình khỏi mã nguồn, dễ dàng quản lý cho các môi trường khác nhau (dev, test, prod).\",\"level\":3},{\"title\":\"2. Quản lý Dependencies Hiệu Quả\",\"description\":\"Hướng dẫn sử dụng BOM (Bill of Materials) của Spring Boot, tránh xung đột phiên bản, và giữ cho các thư viện luôn được cập nhật để đảm bảo tính ổn định và bảo mật.\",\"level\":3},{\"title\":\"3. Xử lý Ngoại Lệ Toàn Cục (Global Exception Handling)\",\"description\":\"Sử dụng `@ControllerAdvice` và `@ExceptionHandler` để xử lý các ngoại lệ một cách tập trung, trả về phản hồi nhất quán và thân thiện với người dùng cho client.\",\"level\":3},{\"title\":\"4. Ghi Log Đúng Cách (Effective Logging)\",\"description\":\"Sử dụng SLF4J/Logback, cấu hình mức độ log (INFO, DEBUG, ERROR), ghi log có cấu trúc (structured logging) và tránh ghi log thông tin nhạy cảm để dễ dàng debug và giám sát.\",\"level\":3},{\"title\":\"5. Bảo mật với Spring Security (Spring Security Best Practices)\",\"description\":\"Cấu hình xác thực (authentication) và ủy quyền (authorization), sử dụng JWT/OAuth2, bảo vệ CSRF, và các biện pháp bảo mật cơ bản khác để bảo vệ ứng dụng khỏi các mối đe dọa.\",\"level\":3},{\"title\":\"6. Kiểm tra Đơn vị và Tích hợp (Unit & Integration Testing)\",\"description\":\"Nhấn mạnh tầm quan trọng của việc viết test, sử dụng JUnit, Mockito, Spring Boot Test để đảm bảo chất lượng mã nguồn, phát hiện lỗi sớm và duy trì ứng dụng.\",\"level\":3},{\"title\":\"7. Tối ưu Hóa Cơ sở Dữ liệu (Database Optimization)\",\"description\":\"Sử dụng Connection Pooling (HikariCP), tối ưu hóa truy vấn JPA/Hibernate, tránh N+1 problem, và quản lý transaction hiệu quả để cải thiện hiệu suất truy cập dữ liệu.\",\"level\":3},{\"title\":\"8. Sử dụng Actuator để Giám sát (Monitoring with Actuator)\",\"description\":\"Kích hoạt và cấu hình Spring Boot Actuator để giám sát sức khỏe ứng dụng, metrics, thông tin môi trường, giúp dễ dàng theo dõi và khắc phục sự cố.\",\"level\":3},{\"title\":\"9. Quản lý Phiên (Session Management)\",\"description\":\"Sử dụng Spring Session cho các ứng dụng phân tán, tránh lưu trữ trạng thái trong bộ nhớ cục bộ của server để đảm bảo khả năng mở rộng và chịu lỗi.\",\"level\":3},{\"title\":\"10. Xử lý Bất đồng bộ (Asynchronous Processing)\",\"description\":\"Sử dụng `@Async` để thực hiện các tác vụ tốn thời gian ở chế độ nền, cải thiện khả năng phản hồi của ứng dụng và trải nghiệm người dùng.\",\"level\":3},{\"title\":\"11. Validation Dữ liệu Đầu vào (Input Data Validation)\",\"description\":\"Sử dụng Bean Validation (JSR 380) với `@Valid` và `@Validated` để đảm bảo tính toàn vẹn và hợp lệ của dữ liệu nhận được từ client, ngăn chặn lỗi và các cuộc tấn công.\",\"level\":3},{\"title\":\"12. Sử dụng Docker và Containerization\",\"description\":\"Đóng gói ứng dụng Spring Boot vào Docker image nhỏ gọn, tối ưu hóa Dockerfile cho môi trường production để dễ dàng triển khai và quản lý.\",\"level\":3},{\"title\":\"13. Quản lý Cache (Caching Strategies)\",\"description\":\"Áp dụng caching với `@Cacheable`, `@CachePut`, `@CacheEvict` để cải thiện hiệu suất, giảm tải cho cơ sở dữ liệu và các dịch vụ backend khác, sử dụng các giải pháp cache như Ehcache, Redis.\",\"level\":3},{\"title\":\"14. Xử lý Graceful Shutdown\",\"description\":\"Đảm bảo ứng dụng có thể tắt một cách an toàn mà không làm mất dữ liệu hoặc gián đoạn các yêu cầu đang xử lý, đặc biệt quan trọng trong môi trường microservices.\",\"level\":3},{\"title\":\"15. Tối ưu hóa Kích thước JAR (JAR Size Optimization)\",\"description\":\"Sử dụng `spring-boot-thin-launcher` hoặc loại bỏ các dependencies không cần thiết để giảm kích thước file JAR, tăng tốc độ triển khai và giảm tài nguyên sử dụng.\",\"level\":3},{\"title\":\"Kết luận\",\"description\":\"Tóm tắt các điểm chính và khuyến khích độc giả áp dụng các thực hành tốt này để xây dựng các ứng dụng Spring Boot mạnh mẽ, ổn định và sẵn sàng cho production.\",\"level\":2}]}","responseLength":4644,"tokenUsage":{"inputTokens":423,"outputTokens":1504,"totalTokens":3508},"estimatedCost":0.000966,"promptName":"planner","promptVersion":"v1","retryCount":0}
